# Philosophy and Goals

## Why technical GRC

Governance, risk and compliance has a reputation for being paperwork: policies, spreadsheets and audit evidence collected once a year. A lot of it is, and that part matters. But the controls in those documents only mean something if there is a system underneath that actually does the thing.

Take a control that says "monitor systems for anomalous activity". Behind that sentence there are choices someone has to make: which logs get collected, where they go, what counts as unusual, who gets alerted, and what record shows it worked. A person who understands both the requirement and the system can connect the two. That is the kind of GRC work I want to be able to do.

So my approach is to learn the technical side of a control (how it's built in a cloud environment, what telemetry it produces) next to the framework side (what the standard requires, how an auditor will read it).

## Why AI governance, and why agents

Most AI governance talk is about models: bias, transparency, and what a model says. That matters, but agentic AI changes the problem. An agent doesn't just produce text. It reads input, decides what to do, calls tools and takes actions, often without a human approving each step.

When software acts on its own, an old security question gets sharper: **who is it acting as, and what is it allowed to do?** Every action an agent takes runs under some credential. That makes agent governance in large part an identity and monitoring problem, which are two things security teams already know how to work on. I think that's a useful way into a field that can otherwise feel vague.

The honest state of things is that the existing frameworks (ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2, NIST AI RMF, the EU AI Act) weren't written with autonomous agents in mind, and agent-specific guidance is still emerging. I find that gap interesting, not discouraging, and I write about it directly instead of pretending the controls line up neatly.

## How I work

- **Primary sources first.** I read the standard, the regulator's page or the vendor documentation, and link it.
- **Say what's quoted and what's interpreted.** When I map a framework to a technical control, I label the mapping as my reading.
- **Flag uncertainty.** If I couldn't verify something, I say so in the note.
- **Build the thing.** Notes are useful, but I learn more from doing a hands-on project, such as writing a Statement of Applicability for a hypothetical company and justifying every choice.
- **Write to learn.** Publishing on Substack forces me to explain things clearly, and the feedback shows me where I'm wrong.

## Goals

These are the directions I'm working toward. They'll change as I learn more.

**Now**
- Finish studying for Microsoft's SC-900 (Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals) and keep my notes as a reference
- Build a few applied GRC projects, starting with an ISO/IEC 27001 Statement of Applicability for a hypothetical AI startup
- Keep publishing: one clearly argued piece at a time

**Next**
- Get hands-on with Microsoft Sentinel and Defender, so my notes on monitoring and incident response come from practice and not only from documentation
- Go deeper on the standards that show up in real audits (ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2) and how AI systems fit into them
- Follow how agent-specific guidance develops (NIST's AI agent work, OWASP, industry groups) and update my notes as it does

**Longer term**
- Work in a technical GRC or AI governance role, where the job is connecting what the framework requires to what the system does
- Be someone who can explain a control to an engineer and an auditor, and have both understand it

## What this portfolio is not

It's not a claim of expertise I don't have, and it's not a substitute for professional advice. It's a record of how I'm learning, and I hope it's also useful to other people learning the same things.

